{"slug": "rtd-resistance-to-temperature-calculator", "targets": ["pt1000 formula", "pt100 ohm to celsius calculator"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">PT1000 Formula and Quick Ohm to Celsius Reference</h2><p><strong>PT1000 formula</strong> for 0 °C and above is Rt = 1000 × (1 + A × T + B × T²), with the IEC 60751 coefficients A = 3.9083 × 10⁻³ and B = minus 5.775 × 10⁻⁷. It is the same Callendar Van Dusen equation as the PT100, only R0 is 1000 ohm instead of 100 ohm, so every PT1000 resistance is ten times the PT100 value.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Use</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">PT1000 formula</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Temperature to resistance (T ≥ 0 °C)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Rt = 1000 × (1 + A × T + B × T²)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Resistance to temperature (Rt ≥ 1000 ohm)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">T = [minus A + √(A² minus 4 × B × (1 minus Rt/1000))] ÷ (2 × B)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Quick linear estimate</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">T ≈ (Rt minus 1000) ÷ 3.85</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">PT100 and PT1000 Ohm to Celsius Table</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Temperature (°C)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">PT100 (ohm)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">PT1000 (ohm)</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100.00</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1000.0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">50</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">119.40</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1194.0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">138.51</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1385.1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">150</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">157.33</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1573.3</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">200</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">175.86</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1758.6</td></tr></table></div><p>Use the table as a manual PT100 ohm to celsius calculator for spot checks: if a PT1000 reads 1385.1 ohm, the probe is at 100 °C. Below 0 °C the C term must be added, so use the calculator above for negative temperatures.</p>"}